:{$PORT:8080}

# This listener serves public traffic AND SvelteKit SSR's own API calls, which
# enter on 127.0.0.1 and are routed to Django by the @django handle below. Any
# directive added at site level therefore applies to internal SSR traffic too.

# Bunny inherits the pull zone's 30-day default TTL for any response that
# arrives without Cache-Control, so a redirect that ever reached it unstamped
# could be served long after the condition that produced it was fixed. The
# `>` form matters: see the note above `header ?Cache-Control` below.
@www host www.flipcommons.org
header @www >Cache-Control "no-store"
handle @www {
	redir https://flipcommons.org{uri} permanent
}

# Send anything that reaches the Railway origin hostname without passing
# through Bunny to the public origin instead. Crawlers found that hostname on
# their own and were indexing it as a second copy of the site; direct traffic
# was 13% of everything reaching this container, none of it cached, rate
# limited or fronted by anything. A 301 rather than a 403: these URLs are
# duplicates of live pages, so the accrued signal should move to the apex
# rather than be discarded.
#
# Bunny's own pulls carry this hostname too, so `header !X-Origin-Auth` is the
# only thing keeping them out of this matcher. Without it every pull would be
# redirected to a host Bunny fronts, and with Follow Redirects disabled that
# loop would be cached at the edge and outlive the repair. Testing the
# header's presence rather than its value keeps a drifted ORIGIN_SHARED_SECRET
# surfacing as the plain 403 the gate below produces. Neither condition is a
# security control; the gate is.
#
# Nothing internal matches: SSR's own API calls arrive as 127.0.0.1:$PORT and
# Railway's health check as healthcheck.railway.app.
@direct_origin {
	host flipcommons-production.up.railway.app
	header !X-Origin-Auth
}
# Bunny inherits the pull zone's 30-day default TTL for any response that
# arrives without Cache-Control, so an unstamped redirect could be cached and
# served long after the condition that produced it was fixed.
header @direct_origin >Cache-Control "no-store"
handle @direct_origin {
	redir https://flipcommons.org{uri} permanent
}

# Refuse anything that did not come through Bunny: the apex zone's Edge Rule
# stamps every origin pull with X-Origin-Auth, so a request without the right
# value bypassed the edge cache and its rate limits, whatever Host it wears.
# Exempt are SSR's own API calls, which arrive on loopback, and Railway's
# health probe. The probe exemption pairs its Host with the path on purpose:
# a /__health request under the public host is Bunny forwarding the uptime
# monitor, which must carry the secret like everything else so that a
# drifted secret turns the monitor red rather than 403ing the site behind a
# green health check.
#
# The :__origin_secret_unset__ default is load-bearing. Caddy substitutes
# {$VAR} into the raw file before parsing, so an unset variable would leave a
# bare `header X-Origin-Auth`, which matches any value. The sentinel makes an
# unset secret reject everything instead. Same expression as @trusted_cdn.
@unauthenticated {
	not header X-Origin-Auth {$ORIGIN_SHARED_SECRET:__origin_secret_unset__}
	not remote_ip 127.0.0.1 ::1
	not {
		host healthcheck.railway.app
		path /__health
	}
}
header @unauthenticated >Cache-Control "no-store"
handle @unauthenticated {
	respond 403
}

# Strip the RFC 7239 Forwarded header. Railway passes client-supplied
# values through verbatim, so it's attacker-controlled.
request_header -Forwarded

# Real client IP behind the Bunny apex edge cache. When Bunny fronts the
# apex, Railway's edge rebuilds X-Forwarded-For and X-Real-IP from its
# immediate peer (Bunny), so the true client IP is lost from the standard
# headers. The Bunny pull zone re-injects it via an Edge Rule
# (X-Client-IP=%{User.IP}), paired with a shared-secret X-Origin-Auth header —
# both custom headers, which Railway forwards verbatim. We promote
# X-Client-IP to X-Real-IP (the rate limiter's single sanctioned reader) ONLY
# when X-Origin-Auth matches the secret, so a direct *.railway.app hit can't
# spoof it. ORIGIN_SHARED_SECRET is a deployment contract (like
# RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS); the sentinel default never matches real
# traffic when the env is unset.
@trusted_cdn header X-Origin-Auth {$ORIGIN_SHARED_SECRET:__origin_secret_unset__}
request_header @trusted_cdn X-Real-IP {http.request.header.X-Client-IP}

# Response-side security headers. CSP is owned by SvelteKit
# (svelte.config.js `kit.csp`) because it needs a per-page hash/nonce
# for the inline hydration script — Caddy can't synthesize that. Django
# paths (/api/, /djadmin/, /static/, /media/) are intentionally left
# without CSP: /api/ and /media/ don't render HTML, /static/ is already-
# bundled assets, and /djadmin/ has inline scripts that wouldn't
# tolerate a strict policy. X-Frame-Options below still applies site-
# wide as belt-and-suspenders against framing (CSP frame-ancestors
# covers modern browsers). Referrer-Policy here overwrites Django's
# stricter same-origin default on /djadmin/ — accepted because the
# public site needs strict-origin-when-cross-origin for analytics
# attribution. HSTS lives here rather than Django; see
# docs/Hosting.md § HSTS.
header {
	X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
	Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
	X-Frame-Options DENY
	Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=86400"
}

# Every response leaving this container carries an explicit Cache-Control,
# because Bunny gives anything without one its 30-day zone default. The
# SvelteKit hook (frontend/src/lib/cache-control.server.ts) stamps what it
# sees, but it never sees prerendered pages, files under frontend/static/ or
# /_app/env.js; only Caddy sees every response. The default is `private,
# no-store` so a prerendered page such as /privacy is re-served by Node
# rather than frozen in Bunny for 30 days after its text changes. The 502
# Caddy writes itself when it cannot dial an upstream is the one response
# these lines never reach; handle_errors at the bottom covers it.
header ?Cache-Control "private, no-store"

# Every matcher-bound Cache-Control in this file uses the deferred `>` form,
# for two reasons. A plain `header` runs before reverse_proxy, and the proxy
# then copies upstream headers with Add, so on a response the hook also
# stamped (a 404 under /images/, the /__health probe) the two values would
# stack as two Cache-Control headers; `>` sets at write time and replaces.
# And a deferred op replaces a plain one that ran earlier, so a plain
# `no-store` on the 403 or a redirect would be overwritten by the font or
# image policy on those paths, and a PoP that pulled a font during a secret
# rotation would cache the 403 for a year. Deferred ops are response-writer
# wrappers that unwind innermost first, so the earliest line applies last
# and wins. The cost is that a 404 on an unfingerprinted path is cached for
# the path's TTL, which only bites during a rename.

# Unfingerprinted files under frontend/static/ that are referenced by fixed
# path: favicons, logos, portraits, the web manifest. An hour in the browser
# and a day at the edge bounds how long a replaced file stays stale without
# a purge. Fonts are below with their own longer policy.
@static_files path /images/* /apple-touch-icon.png /site.webmanifest /fakes/*
header @static_files >Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=86400"

# SvelteKit answers /_app/env.js itself, before its hooks run, with only an
# ETag. It carries the $env/dynamic/public values, including the Sentry
# release tag, so an unstamped copy would freeze the release for 30 days at
# each PoP. Browsers revalidate on every load; the edge holds it a minute.
@env path /_app/env.js
header @env >Cache-Control "public, max-age=0, s-maxage=60"

# Long-cache the self-hosted fonts. SvelteKit's Node adapter serves
# files from frontend/static/ without cache headers because they're
# unfingerprinted; we know these specific bytes are stable, so override.
# `immutable` means browsers will NOT revalidate — if Lora is ever
# updated, the file MUST be renamed (e.g. lora-latin-v2.woff2) or
# cached clients will never see the new version.
@fonts path /fonts/*
header @fonts >Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

# Short TTL on the deploy-version probe. SvelteKit writes the current
# SHA to this path and the client polls it hourly (svelte.config.js
# kit.version.pollInterval) to detect deploys. Without an explicit
# header, Bunny applies its 30-day zone default and open tabs would
# miss new deploys until the cache expires. The static zone pulls this
# through the apex zone, so the TTL applies at each hop and a tab can
# see a deploy up to two TTLs late.
#
# CORS headers are required because the poll runs cross-origin once
# kit.paths.assets points at the CDN — see node_modules/@sveltejs/kit
# runtime/client/utils.js, which fetches `${assets}/_app/version.json`
# with `pragma` and `cache-control` request headers. Both are non-
# safelisted, which forces a browser preflight; without ACAO + an
# Allow-Headers entry covering them, the preflight fails and version
# polling silently breaks in every tab. Bunny passes preflights through
# to Caddy verbatim, so handling them here is sufficient — the
# dedicated handle below short-circuits OPTIONS to a 204 rather than
# letting SvelteKit serve the full body on a preflight.
@version path /_app/version.json
header @version >Cache-Control "public, max-age=60"
header @version {
	Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
	Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Cache-Control, Pragma"
	Access-Control-Max-Age "7200"
}
@version_preflight {
	method OPTIONS
	path /_app/version.json
}
handle @version_preflight {
	respond 204
}

# Never cache the liveness probe. Uptime monitoring has to observe the
# origin's current state, and an edge PoP answering probes from cache
# would report the app up while it is down. The SvelteKit hook stamps
# this endpoint too; this line is the Caddy-side statement of the same
# policy, so the probe stays uncacheable if the hook's rules change.
@health path /__health
header @health >Cache-Control "no-store"

# XFF rewrite must live inside reverse_proxy: Caddy's reverse_proxy has
# special handling for X-Forwarded-* headers and overrides any
# site-level request_header mutations. header_up is proxy-aware and
# wins. Duplicated across the two reverse_proxy blocks because Caddyfile
# snippets must be defined at global scope (before any site address),
# which is awkward to thread here for a single-line directive.
#
# This block is also SSR's route to the API: INTERNAL_API_BASE_URL points
# Node at this listener rather than at Gunicorn, so Node sees a keep-alive
# upstream. scripts/start-production owns the rationale, and the contract is
# pinned by backend/tests/test_ssr_api_route.py. Narrowing the /api match, or
# moving Django's proxy out of this site, breaks SSR — not just public
# /api callers.
@django path /api /api/* /djadmin /djadmin/* /media/* /static /static/*
handle @django {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{$DJANGO_PORT:8000} {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.header.X-Real-IP}
	}
}

handle {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{$NODE_PORT:3000} {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.header.X-Real-IP}
	}
}

# When reverse_proxy cannot dial Node or Gunicorn (a restart, a crash), Caddy
# writes the 502 itself, outside the response-writer wrappers the deferred
# header ops live in, so neither the `?` default nor any `>` policy above
# reaches it. Without this block that 502 would arrive at Bunny bare and
# inherit the 30-day zone default. The status is preserved; the body is the
# code, which is what an uptime monitor or a curl needs and nothing more.
handle_errors {
	header >Cache-Control "no-store"
	respond "{err.status_code}" {err.status_code}
}
